- yash-random-y-tst-hangs: `tests/yash/random-y.tst` hangs (bounded by
  CTest's 120s per-file TIMEOUT). The rest of the suite is fine.
  `$RANDOM` itself varies correctly when checked directly, so the
  two busy-`until`-loops at the top aren't the obvious cause; more
  likely the later cases piping a function's output through subshells
  (`print > seeded1; print > seeded2; diff seeded1 seeded2`), similar
  in shape to `subshell-exit-trap-output-misdirected`. Not isolated to
  a specific line yet.

  2026-07-29: confirmed still open and unrelated to any in-tree
  change -- reproduces identically on a from-scratch checkout of this
  exact commit, with no local modifications. The failure is genuinely
  build/timing-sensitive: a `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release` build of
  `shish` ran this file to completion (with its normal 3 case
  failures) across several repeated direct
  `sh tests/run-tst.sh ./shish tests/yash random-y.tst` invocations,
  while the project's *default* `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=MinSizeRel` build
  hangs on it almost every time, both under `ctest -R yash/random-y.tst`
  and via direct `run-tst.sh` invocation. Whatever's wrong reads like
  undefined behavior whose effect flips with optimization level/stack
  layout, not a straightforward logic bug -- the same flavor of bug as
  `redir-p.tst`'s builtin_cat.c landmine below, though not confirmed
  to be the same root cause. The hung process sits at 100% CPU (a
  spin, not a blocked syscall), which does point back at the two
  busy-`until` `$RANDOM`-comparison loops at the top of the file after
  all, despite the earlier note ruling them out from a shallower
  check -- next step is bisecting with a debug build (`cfg -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug`)
  attached via a debugger to catch exactly which loop is spinning and
  why the two `$RANDOM` reads it compares aren't diverging.

- redir-fd-chain-resolves-to-invalid-fd: `tests/posix/redir-p.tst`'s
  "file descriptors up to 9 are supported" case --
  `cat 9<in0 8<&9 7<&8 6<&7 5<&6 4<&5 3<&4 0<&3` -- on a
  `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release` build specifically (the project's
  default `MinSizeRel` build runs this file to completion, with its
  normal handful of unrelated case failures, every time) this 8-deep
  `<&` dup chain resolves `fd_in`'s effective fd to -1 (confirmed via
  `strace`: `cat`'s first read comes back `EBADF`). Not yet isolated
  past that -- `fdtable_resolve.c`'s `fd_expected`/`fdtable_dup()`
  interaction is the likely area, per its own comments about
  `fd_expected` going stale, but unconfirmed. Confirmed present on a
  from-scratch checkout with zero local changes, so unrelated to any
  in-tree work in progress; reads like undefined behavior whose effect
  depends on optimization level/stack layout rather than a
  straightforward logic bug -- possibly the same underlying cause as
  `yash-random-y-tst-hangs` above, though unconfirmed. (Used to hang
  the shell forever instead of just misbehaving --
  `builtin_cat()` had no handling for a negative `buffer_get_until()`
  return and looped calling it forever; fixed separately, fixes/88.)
